# profiler
  -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  What happens inside profile_cluster() step by step
  -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

  Step 1 — Node layout discovery

  Every rank has two env vars set by torchrun:
  - LOCAL_RANK: this rank's index within its node (0, 1, 2, ...)
  - LOCAL_WORLD_SIZE: how many GPUs are on this node

  Each rank packs these into a 2-element tensor and calls dist.all_gather. After the gather, every rank knows the (LOCAL_RANK, LOCAL_WORLD_SIZE) of all 8 ranks:

  global_rank | LOCAL_RANK | LOCAL_WORLD_SIZE | node
       0      |     0      |        2         | node18 ← LOCAL_RANK resets to 0 → new node
       1      |     1      |        2         | node18
       2      |     0      |        4         | node20 ← reset → new node
       3      |     1      |        4         | node20
       4      |     2      |        4         | node20
       5      |     3      |        4         | node20
       6      |     0      |        2         | node16 ← reset → new node
       7      |     1      |        2         | node16

  Result: nodes = [[0,1], [2,3,4,5], [6,7]]

  Step 2 — Pair selection

  intra_pair = (0, 1)   ← first two ranks on node18 (same PCIe bus)
  cross_pair = (0, 2)   ← rank 0 on node18 → rank 2 on node20 (100 GbE)

  Step 3 — ALL ranks call dist.new_group() (mandatory)

  intra_pg = dist.new_group([0, 1])    # all 8 ranks call this
  cross_pg = dist.new_group([0, 2])    # all 8 ranks call this

  Ranks not in the group get a handle but never use it. PyTorch requires this.

  Step 4 — Measuring α/β by linear regression

  For the intra pair (0, 1), rank 0 sends tensors of 7 sizes:

  size=   1 KB  → send 10 times → avg = T₁
  size=   4 KB  → send 10 times → avg = T₂
  size=  16 KB  → T₃
  size=  64 KB  → T₄
  size= 256 KB  → T₅
  size=   1 MB  → T₆
  size=   4 MB  → T₇

  Then fit: T = α + β × S using least-squares.

             T (µs)
      ↑
   T₇ •                              ← slope = β (inverse bandwidth)
      |                        •  T₆
      |              •  T₅
      |        •  T₄
      |     •  T₃
   T₁ • •                            ← y-intercept = α (latency)
      └──────────────────────────────→  S (bytes)
      0   256KB   1MB   2MB   4MB

  All other 6 ranks do nothing during this measurement (no send/recv).

  Step 5 — Measuring T_block

  Every rank independently runs forward + backward through a single transformer block (the _OneBlock class defined inline). Uses torch.cuda.Event for GPU-accurate timing — no CPU-side time.perf_counter() inaccuracy:

  start.record()            # GPU timestamp
  loss = model(x).sum()     # forward
  loss.backward()           # backward
  end.record()              # GPU timestamp
  torch.cuda.synchronize()  # wait for GPU to finish
  T_block = start.elapsed_time(end) / 1000.0  # ms → seconds

  Step 6 — Synchronise via all_reduce MAX

  rank 0: α_intra=5.2µs, β_intra=1.1ns/B, α_cross=0, β_cross=0, T_block=1.2ms
  rank 2: α_intra=0,     β_intra=0,        α_cross=82µs, β_cross=79ns/B, T_block=1.3ms
  rank 4: all zeros except T_block=1.1ms
  ...

  After all_reduce(MAX):
  every rank: α_intra=5.2µs, β_intra=1.1ns/B, α_cross=82µs, β_cross=79ns/B, T_block=1.3ms


